extends Node3D

## The Neon Marble Machine visual proof: start platform, mixing bowl, bridge.
##
## A sibling of `race_scene.gd`, not a change to it. The two play the same
## replay format through the same offline renderer and keep the same contract
## - Godot is presentation only, every position comes out of the file, nothing
## here runs physics or race rules - and they disagree about everything else,
## because they are answers to different questions.
##
## `race_scene.gd` draws whatever course it is handed. It has to: it plays
## courses it has never seen, so it recognises a piece by its role and its
## angle and can know nothing about what the course *is*. That generality is
## exactly what caps how three-dimensional it can look. A funnel drawn as a
## set of angled beams is a set of angled beams.
##
## This file draws one machine, and is told where its parts are. The course
## exports a centre, a radius and a handful of planes in `course.metadata`,
## and the bowl mesh, the presentation heights and the camera are all built
## from those numbers. That is what buys the third dimension: a bowl can be a
## bowl instead of two ramps, because something knows it is a bowl.
##
## ## The presentation mapping
##
## The simulation is two-dimensional and is never touched. Simulation x
## becomes world X and simulation y becomes world Z, exactly as in the race
## scene - one to one, no warp, so two racers touching in the simulation are
## touching on screen and no pair can be drawn intersecting that is not
## intersecting.
##
## The whole of the third axis is derived, by one function:
##
##     deck_height(sim_x, sim_y) -> world Y
##
##         start        flat, the highest surface in the machine
##         chute        eased descent to the bowl rim
##         bowl         H_RIM - BOWL_DEPTH * profile(rho)
##         throat       eased descent below the drain
##         bridge       gentle descent, and a shallow finish beyond it
##
## `rho` is the racer's distance from the bowl centre in the simulation
## plane, over the bowl radius. That is the whole trick of the bowl: a racer
## far from the centre is high on the wall, a racer near the centre is at the
## floor, and a marble that shoots across the basin and up the other side
## does on screen exactly what a marble in a bowl does. Free fall inside the
## disc reads as a slide down the inner surface, because falling reduces the
## distance to the centre.
##
## Every surface in the machine is generated by evaluating the same function
## the racers are placed by. That is not tidiness - it is the only way to
## guarantee the acceptance criterion. A racer sits at `deck_height` plus its
## radius and the mesh under it *is* `deck_height`, so a racer can never
## float above the bowl or sink into it however the physics behaves.
##
## ## Everything is a pure function of the replay tick
##
## As in the race scene: the offline renderer seeks to `frame / fps` and
## draws. Nothing here integrates, accumulates or randomises. The camera's
## framing, the shutter's animation and the racer transforms are all computed
## from the playhead, so two renders of one replay are identical.
##
## ## What V1.1 changed
##
## The mapping above is untouched - it was the thing V1 set out to prove and it
## proved. What this revision changes is everything the mapping is dressed in,
## and each piece carries its own argument at the function that builds it:
##
##     _build_start_platform   four loading bays over four launch channels,
##                             replacing one nine-hundred-pixel apron
##     _build_bowl             an acrylic wall standing *on* the rim with the
##                             near side cut away, a machined rim, a cradle
##                             outside the flange, and a window into the throat
##     _build_room             a hall at three distances instead of a void
##     _build_ribbon           a five-value cross-section on a deep girder
##     _support_positions      supports at the ends and the apexes, three
##                             designs, splayed clear of the deck
##
## `docs/neon_machine_visual_polish_v11.md` has the measurements, the camera
## comparison, the country verdict and the real limitations.

const NeonMaterials := preload("res://scripts/neon_materials.gd")
const NeonFlags := preload("res://scripts/neon_flags.gd")

# The single place where logical simulation pixels become Godot world units.
# The same figure every other scene in this project uses.
const PIXELS_PER_UNIT := 100.0

# --- presentation heights -------------------------------------------------
#
# In world units, and the reason the machine has a silhouette at all. The
# brief asks for three sections at visibly different heights; these are those
# heights, and the nine units between the platform deck and the end of the
# bridge are what a tilted lens turns into a stack of levels.
#
# They were half this to begin with and the machine read as one continuous
# pale slope from the deck to the bowl - a ten degree ramp is a floor, not a
# level change. At the drop below, the chute leaves the platform at
# twenty-three degrees and has a lit edge, a thickness and a shadow of its
# own, and the bowl sits visibly beneath the platform rather than beside it.
const H_START := 0.0
const H_RIM := -2.60
const BOWL_DEPTH := 2.60
const H_THROAT := -6.20
const H_BRIDGE_END := -9.20
const H_FINISH_DROP := 0.55

# The bowl profile, in bowl-local radius.
#
# `FLOOR_RHO` is where the flat floor around the drain ends and the wall
# begins to rise. It is wider than the drain itself so a racer waiting its
# turn at the hole stands on a floor rather than balancing on a slope.
#
# `FLANGE_OUTER` is the outer edge of the flat lip, and it exists because a
# racer arriving over the rim is at a radius slightly past 1.0 - the measured
# worst case on the reference replay is 1.11 - and every one of those has to
# have something under it. Anything further out is clamped onto the lip.
const FLOOR_RHO := 0.26
const FLANGE_OUTER := 1.20
const BOWL_PROFILE_POWER := 1.9

